IMPORT prelude

-- Legislation Act 2001 (Australian Capital Territory): the kinds of Act and instrument that the Act names.
--
-- The other layer-1 modules (except the time module, which stands alone) use this type for "an Act", "a subordinate law" and so on.
-- Each chapter of the Act that says what "law" means for that chapter picks some of these kinds; those choices are written in the chapter's module.

§ `Legislation Act 2001 (ACT): nouns`

-- ss 7 to 13: an Act is an Act of the Legislative Assembly (s 7);
-- a statutory instrument includes a subordinate law, disallowable instrument, notifiable instrument and commencement notice (s 13 (2)).
-- `a statutory instrument of another kind` is a statutory instrument that is none of those four.
-- src:195 | (2) A statutory instrument includes a subordinate law, disallowable instrument, notifiable instrument and commencement notice.
@ref s 13 (2) (src:195-195)
DECLARE `Kind of Act or instrument` IS ONE OF
    `an Act`
    `a subordinate law`
    `a disallowable instrument`
    `a notifiable instrument`
    `a commencement notice`
    `a statutory instrument of another kind`

-- s 130: a definition is a provision that gives a meaning to a term, or limits or extends the meaning of a term.
-- The forms below are those of the examples in s 130 (means; includes; means and includes; a signpost definition "X—see ..."; any other form).
-- A `Definition` record holds the term, the form, where the definition applies, the first line of the definition as the text prints it,
-- and the further lines of the definition (paragraphs and the like) as the text prints them.
-- src:1644 | A definition is a provision (however expressed) of an Act or statutory instrument that—
-- src:1645 | (a) gives a meaning to a term; or
-- src:1646 | (b) limits or extends the meaning of a term.
@ref s 130 (src:1644-1646)
DECLARE `How a term is defined` IS ONE OF
    `means`
    `includes`
    `means and includes`
    `a signpost definition`
    `another form of definition`

DECLARE `Definition` HAS
    `term` IS A STRING
    `how it is defined` IS A `How a term is defined`
    `applies to` IS A STRING
    `meaning as printed` IS A STRING
    `further lines as printed` IS A LIST OF STRING
